```
chain FORWARD {
    iifname "eth2" ip saddr 172.16.10.0/24 ip daddr 172.18.51.1 tcp dport 80 accept
    iifname "eth2" meta l4proto tcp drop
    iifname "eth2" meta l4proto udp drop
    iifname "eth2" counter packets 30 bytes 2520 drop   ← ICMP & everything else DROPPED
}
```

- eth2 on router6 = `r6_r3` (the uplink to router3 / rest of network)
- **30 packets/2520 bytes** already dropped — actively firing
- pc15→172.18.51.254 (router6.eth1): ✅ 0% loss — local gateway reachable
- pc15→10.50.1.1 (router3, requires FORWARD via eth2): ❌ 100% loss — confirmed drop

This ACL severs all transit through router6.eth2: packets from 172.16.10.x/192.168.200.x arrive at router6.eth2 and are dropped before reaching the 172.18.51.x side; return traffic from 172.18.51.x hosts (via router6→router3→...→destinations) also enters router6 back on eth2 and is dropped.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** An nftables FORWARD chain firewall rule on router6's inter-router interface eth2 (the `r6_r3` link connecting router6 to router3 and, transitively, to the rest of the network).

**Mechanism:** The FORWARD chain on router6 contains three consecutive drop rules keyed on `iifname "eth2"`: one drops TCP, one drops UDP, and a final catch-all drops everything else (including ICMP). The only exemption is a single accept rule for TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1. Because eth2 is the sole transit link between the 172.18.51.x/172.18.50.x segment (served by router6) and the broader topology (via router3), **all routed traffic in either direction must cross this interface**, and virtually all of it is silently dropped.

**Localization:** The fault is entirely on **router6**, in its nftables `filter` table, `FORWARD` chain. The counter on the final drop rule (`packets 30 bytes 2520`) confirms active drops. All other routers have healthy RIP-populated routing tables; mswitch2, switch10, and router6's local interfaces (eth0/eth1) are fully operational, which is why intra-172.18.51.x traffic (e.g., pc15↔pc16) works normally.

**Root cause:** A stateless nftables ACL on router6 drops all forwarded traffic entering via eth2, effectively isolating the entire 172.18.51.x segment from the rest of the network in both directions.

**Fix:** Remove the offending `iifname "eth2"` drop rules from router6's nftables FORWARD chain (or flush the chain entirely if the topology requires unrestricted forwarding): `nft flush chain ip filter FORWARD`.
=== END ===